1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,591
Cross-Text Connections questions give
you two short texts on the same topic,

2
00:00:04,611 --> 00:00:06,377
then ask how they connect.

3
00:00:06,397 --> 00:00:11,090
How would the author of Text two
respond to a claim in Text one?

4
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:14,672
How would they describe
a view in Text one?

5
00:00:14,692 --> 00:00:18,493
The common mistake is
understanding each text on its own,

6
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:21,335
but never deciding how they fit together.

7
00:00:21,585 --> 00:00:25,922
In this video: a two-step
method, five relationships,

8
00:00:25,942 --> 00:00:31,335
six original SAT-style questions,
and the four traps to watch for.

9
00:00:31,366 --> 00:00:32,203
They're easy to spot.

10
00:00:32,223 --> 00:00:35,596
You'll see Text one and Text
two stacked on the screen,

11
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:38,988
and the question usually
starts with based on the texts.

12
00:00:39,008 --> 00:00:42,274
They're part of Craft and Structure, the
largest of the four Reading and Writing

13
00:00:42,294 --> 00:00:43,264
domains.

14
00:00:43,284 --> 00:00:44,978
The stem comes in a few forms.

15
00:00:44,998 --> 00:00:50,406
How would the author of Text two most
likely respond to a claim in Text one?

16
00:00:50,426 --> 00:00:53,764
How would they most likely
describe a view in Text one?

17
00:00:53,784 --> 00:00:57,712
And sometimes, what would both
authors most likely agree with?

18
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:01,825
Step one: sum up each text's
main claim in one short line.

19
00:01:01,845 --> 00:01:03,004
Not every detail.

20
00:01:03,024 --> 00:01:04,167
Just the point.

21
00:01:04,238 --> 00:01:06,397
Text one's line, then Text two's line,

22
00:01:06,417 --> 00:01:07,560
side by side.

23
00:01:07,625 --> 00:01:11,284
Step two: name the relationship
between those two lines.

24
00:01:11,304 --> 00:01:12,641
Text two can agree.

25
00:01:12,661 --> 00:01:13,712
It can disagree.

26
00:01:13,732 --> 00:01:17,819
It can qualify, which means it accepts
the main point but adds a limit.

27
00:01:17,839 --> 00:01:21,283
It can extend, with new
evidence or a new case.

28
00:01:21,303 --> 00:01:24,625
Or it can explain something
Text one only describes.

29
00:01:24,669 --> 00:01:27,184
Do both steps before you read the choices.

30
00:01:27,204 --> 00:01:31,556
Then the right answer has to match
the relationship and every detail.

31
00:01:31,576 --> 00:01:32,735
Question one.

32
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:33,861
Text one.

33
00:01:34,075 --> 00:01:38,092
For decades, many scholars argued
that the people of Rapa Nui,

34
00:01:38,112 --> 00:01:42,825
or Easter Island, cut down nearly
all of the island's forests.

35
00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:46,913
In this account, the loss of resources
led to warfare and a collapse of the

36
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:49,556
island's society around sixteen hundred,

37
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:53,933
more than a century before Europeans
arrived in seventeen twenty-two.

38
00:01:54,069 --> 00:01:55,534
Text two.

39
00:01:55,891 --> 00:01:58,784
Some archaeologists now
question this account.

40
00:01:59,034 --> 00:02:03,978
Radiocarbon dates from the stone platforms
that hold the island's famous statues

41
00:02:03,998 --> 00:02:08,764
show that islanders kept building them
at a steady rate until after seventeen

42
00:02:08,784 --> 00:02:09,648
twenty-two.

43
00:02:09,668 --> 00:02:14,683
In their view, the island's sharp
population decline came only after contact,

44
00:02:14,703 --> 00:02:17,953
as disease and slave raids
arrived from outside.

45
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:22,828
Based on the texts, how would the
archaeologists in Text two most likely respond

46
00:02:22,848 --> 00:02:25,257
to the underlined claim in Text one?

47
00:02:25,277 --> 00:02:26,527
Pause and try it.

48
00:02:30,735 --> 00:02:31,822
One line each.

49
00:02:31,842 --> 00:02:35,072
Text one: the society collapsed
around sixteen hundred,

50
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:36,786
before Europeans came.

51
00:02:36,806 --> 00:02:39,000
Text two: they were still building
after seventeen twenty-two,

52
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:41,571
so the decline came later.

53
00:02:41,591 --> 00:02:43,476
That's a disagreement about timing.

54
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:48,261
Choice C: by pointing out that islanders
kept building monument platforms after

55
00:02:48,281 --> 00:02:53,496
seventeen twenty-two, which suggests that
the society had not collapsed by then.

56
00:02:53,602 --> 00:02:56,546
The relationship and
the details both match.

57
00:02:56,566 --> 00:02:58,129
C is the answer.

58
00:02:58,149 --> 00:03:02,343
A just restates Text one, the
view Text two is pushing back on.

59
00:03:02,363 --> 00:03:06,164
B is too extreme, and Text
two never mentions trees.

60
00:03:06,184 --> 00:03:08,164
D gets a detail wrong.

61
00:03:08,184 --> 00:03:12,256
Text two is about the platforms,
not when the statues were carved.

62
00:03:12,285 --> 00:03:13,229
Question two.

63
00:03:13,249 --> 00:03:14,284
Text one.

64
00:03:14,535 --> 00:03:18,122
Each bottlenose dolphin develops
its own distinctive whistle,

65
00:03:18,142 --> 00:03:19,952
called a signature whistle.

66
00:03:19,972 --> 00:03:23,774
Dolphins sometimes copy the signature
whistles of close companions,

67
00:03:23,794 --> 00:03:28,702
and in one experiment, wild dolphins
responded to computer-made copies of their

68
00:03:28,722 --> 00:03:32,793
own signature whistles while
ignoring other dolphins' whistles.

69
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:36,794
Such findings suggest that the
whistles work much like names.

70
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:37,903
Text two.

71
00:03:38,225 --> 00:03:42,133
In twenty twenty-four, a team of
researchers reported that wild African

72
00:03:42,153 --> 00:03:47,475
elephants in Kenya also seem to address
one another with name-like calls.

73
00:03:47,832 --> 00:03:51,919
Elephants responded more strongly to
recordings of calls originally addressed to

74
00:03:51,939 --> 00:03:54,475
them than to calls addressed
to other elephants.

75
00:03:55,291 --> 00:04:00,021
Unlike dolphins, however, the elephants
did not appear to imitate the sounds of

76
00:04:00,041 --> 00:04:01,719
the individual being called.

77
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:07,592
Based on the texts, how would the
researchers in Text two most likely describe

78
00:04:07,612 --> 00:04:10,199
the findings presented in Text one?

79
00:04:10,219 --> 00:04:11,469
Pause and try it.

80
00:04:15,677 --> 00:04:16,907
One line each.

81
00:04:16,927 --> 00:04:20,570
Text one: dolphin
whistles work like names.

82
00:04:20,712 --> 00:04:24,514
Text two: elephants seem to
have name-like calls too,

83
00:04:24,534 --> 00:04:25,871
but they don't imitate.

84
00:04:25,891 --> 00:04:28,335
Text two isn't arguing with Text one.

85
00:04:28,355 --> 00:04:30,728
It carries the idea to a new species.

86
00:04:30,748 --> 00:04:32,040
That's extending.

87
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:37,453
Choice D: as evidence of a name-like
behavior that elephants appear to share,

88
00:04:37,489 --> 00:04:40,503
though the two species
differ in one respect.

89
00:04:40,523 --> 00:04:43,111
That covers the also and the however.

90
00:04:43,131 --> 00:04:44,291
D is the answer.

91
00:04:44,311 --> 00:04:46,061
C is too extreme.

92
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,454
Exactly the same way
contradicts the last sentence.

93
00:04:49,525 --> 00:04:52,505
B says the researchers retested dolphins,

94
00:04:52,525 --> 00:04:54,041
but they studied elephants.

95
00:04:54,061 --> 00:04:57,025
And A invents a disagreement
that isn't there.

96
00:04:57,089 --> 00:04:58,033
Question three.

97
00:04:58,053 --> 00:04:59,033
Text one.

98
00:04:59,053 --> 00:05:03,712
In nineteen fifty-nine, the Soviet
spacecraft Luna three sent back the first

99
00:05:03,732 --> 00:05:07,782
images of the Moon's far side,
and they surprised scientists.

100
00:05:07,802 --> 00:05:10,139
The near side, which always faces Earth,

101
00:05:10,159 --> 00:05:13,212
is marked by broad, dark
plains called maria,

102
00:05:13,232 --> 00:05:15,220
formed by ancient lava flows.

103
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:18,882
The far side, the images
showed, has very few.

104
00:05:18,904 --> 00:05:20,047
Text two.

105
00:05:20,297 --> 00:05:23,706
Measurements of the Moon's gravity
indicate that its crust is thicker,

106
00:05:23,726 --> 00:05:26,990
on average, on the far
side than on the near side.

107
00:05:27,010 --> 00:05:30,562
Many planetary scientists think
this difference is the key:

108
00:05:30,582 --> 00:05:35,206
lava rising from the Moon's interior could
break through to the surface far more

109
00:05:35,226 --> 00:05:37,369
easily where the crust was thin.

110
00:05:37,389 --> 00:05:41,905
Based on the texts, how would the planetary
scientists in Text two most likely

111
00:05:41,925 --> 00:05:45,266
respond to the observation
described in Text one?

112
00:05:45,286 --> 00:05:46,536
Pause and try it.

113
00:05:50,744 --> 00:05:54,010
Text one gives a puzzle: lots
of lava plains on one side,

114
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:55,546
very few on the other.

115
00:05:55,566 --> 00:05:59,617
Text two gives a reason:
thin crust lets lava through,

116
00:05:59,637 --> 00:06:01,958
and the near side's crust is thinner.

117
00:06:02,101 --> 00:06:04,137
So Text two explains Text one.

118
00:06:04,179 --> 00:06:08,802
Choice A: by suggesting that a difference
in crust thickness helps explain why

119
00:06:08,822 --> 00:06:11,552
maria are far more
common on the near side.

120
00:06:11,572 --> 00:06:12,715
That's the answer.

121
00:06:12,745 --> 00:06:13,817
Now look at C.

122
00:06:13,995 --> 00:06:18,797
It also offers an explanation, but it
says the far side has the thinner crust.

123
00:06:18,817 --> 00:06:19,995
That's backward.

124
00:06:20,316 --> 00:06:22,654
This is the trap where
the relationship is right,

125
00:06:22,674 --> 00:06:24,531
but a detail is wrong.

126
00:06:24,566 --> 00:06:27,138
Check every detail against the text.

127
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:32,073
B disagrees with an observation
Text two never questions.

128
00:06:32,109 --> 00:06:34,644
And D is too extreme.

129
00:06:34,859 --> 00:06:37,536
Never isn't supported by either text.

130
00:06:37,565 --> 00:06:39,509
Question four has a different stem.

131
00:06:39,529 --> 00:06:40,529
Text one.

132
00:06:40,743 --> 00:06:44,045
Many of Stonehenge's smaller
stones, known as bluestones,

133
00:06:44,065 --> 00:06:47,580
have been traced to the Preseli
Hills in southwestern Wales,

134
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:50,152
well over a hundred
miles from the monument.

135
00:06:50,172 --> 00:06:54,473
Most archaeologists think Neolithic
people moved these stones the entire

136
00:06:54,493 --> 00:06:58,208
distance themselves, perhaps by
dragging them on wooden sledges.

137
00:06:58,230 --> 00:06:59,444
Text two.

138
00:07:00,051 --> 00:07:02,730
A few geologists have
proposed a different route.

139
00:07:02,873 --> 00:07:07,103
In their view, glaciers during an earlier
ice age carried the bluestones from

140
00:07:07,123 --> 00:07:09,638
Wales much of the way toward the site,

141
00:07:09,658 --> 00:07:13,460
so Stonehenge's builders would have
needed to move the stones only a short

142
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:14,378
distance.

143
00:07:14,398 --> 00:07:18,807
Based on the texts, the archaeologists
in Text one and the geologists in Text

144
00:07:18,827 --> 00:07:22,184
two would most likely
agree with which statement?

145
00:07:22,204 --> 00:07:23,454
Pause and try it.

146
00:07:26,662 --> 00:07:29,070
They disagree about how the stones moved:

147
00:07:29,090 --> 00:07:31,035
people, or glaciers.

148
00:07:31,055 --> 00:07:34,571
But both texts say the
stones came from Wales.

149
00:07:34,591 --> 00:07:37,249
When the question asks
what both would agree with,

150
00:07:37,269 --> 00:07:40,619
look for the common ground
underneath the disagreement.

151
00:07:40,639 --> 00:07:44,583
Choice B: the bluestones originally came
from a place far from where Stonehenge

152
00:07:44,603 --> 00:07:45,405
was built.

153
00:07:45,425 --> 00:07:47,226
Both texts support that.

154
00:07:47,246 --> 00:07:51,262
A is only Text two's view,
and C is only Text one's.

155
00:07:51,282 --> 00:07:53,318
D isn't in either text.

156
00:07:53,346 --> 00:07:54,684
Here are the four traps.

157
00:07:54,704 --> 00:07:57,791
Restating Text one, when
Text two disagrees with it.

158
00:07:57,811 --> 00:08:00,754
Too extreme: never, completely rejects,

159
00:08:00,774 --> 00:08:02,076
exactly the same.

160
00:08:02,096 --> 00:08:04,326
Right relationship, wrong detail.

161
00:08:04,346 --> 00:08:09,146
And swapped texts: giving one author
a view that belongs to the other.

162
00:08:09,166 --> 00:08:12,987
Harder questions usually aren't
simple agreement or disagreement.

163
00:08:13,023 --> 00:08:17,538
Text two often agrees with part of
Text one and pushes back on the rest.

164
00:08:17,558 --> 00:08:18,391
Here's one.

165
00:08:18,411 --> 00:08:19,355
Question five.

166
00:08:19,375 --> 00:08:20,425
Text one.

167
00:08:20,445 --> 00:08:24,676
In studies at a preschool on the Stanford
University campus beginning in the

168
00:08:24,696 --> 00:08:29,461
late nineteen sixties, children could
eat one treat right away or wait alone to

169
00:08:29,481 --> 00:08:30,658
receive two.

170
00:08:30,678 --> 00:08:35,194
Follow-up research found that children
who had waited longer tended to earn

171
00:08:35,214 --> 00:08:40,408
higher SAT scores as teenagers, leading
some researchers to conclude that early

172
00:08:40,428 --> 00:08:42,785
self-control shapes later success.

173
00:08:42,837 --> 00:08:43,924
Text two.

174
00:08:43,944 --> 00:08:48,281
In twenty eighteen, a team of researchers
analyzed results from a similar test

175
00:08:48,301 --> 00:08:51,352
given to a larger, more
diverse group of children.

176
00:08:51,372 --> 00:08:55,710
They, too, found that children who
waited longer tended to score higher on

177
00:08:55,730 --> 00:08:57,758
achievement tests at age fifteen.

178
00:08:57,778 --> 00:09:01,722
But the link was only about half as
strong as in the original studies,

179
00:09:01,742 --> 00:09:06,544
and it shrank by about two thirds once
the team accounted for factors such as

180
00:09:06,564 --> 00:09:08,150
family background.

181
00:09:08,170 --> 00:09:12,687
Based on the texts, how would the researchers
in Text two most likely respond to

182
00:09:12,707 --> 00:09:14,778
the conclusion in Text one?

183
00:09:14,844 --> 00:09:16,094
Pause and try it.

184
00:09:20,301 --> 00:09:21,853
Text two has two halves.

185
00:09:21,873 --> 00:09:23,280
They, too, found a link.

186
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,373
That's agreement.

187
00:09:24,516 --> 00:09:28,816
But the link was weaker, and it shrank
once factors like family background were

188
00:09:28,836 --> 00:09:29,836
counted.

189
00:09:29,873 --> 00:09:30,873
That's a limit.

190
00:09:30,980 --> 00:09:34,766
Agreement plus a limit means
Text two qualifies Text one.

191
00:09:34,786 --> 00:09:37,552
Choice A: by agreeing that a link exists,

192
00:09:37,572 --> 00:09:41,552
but suggesting that much of it may
reflect factors such as family background

193
00:09:41,572 --> 00:09:43,552
rather than self-control alone.

194
00:09:43,572 --> 00:09:44,909
Both halves are there.

195
00:09:44,929 --> 00:09:46,308
A is the answer.

196
00:09:46,328 --> 00:09:48,506
B is the extreme trap.

197
00:09:48,542 --> 00:09:52,094
It says they'd reject the conclusion
because they found no link.

198
00:09:52,114 --> 00:09:53,272
But they did find one.

199
00:09:53,292 --> 00:09:56,414
C goes the other way and
just restates Text one.

200
00:09:56,434 --> 00:09:58,451
And D twists a detail.

201
00:09:58,471 --> 00:10:02,864
Text two never says the finding
only applies to wealthy families.

202
00:10:02,884 --> 00:10:03,971
Last one.

203
00:10:03,991 --> 00:10:05,063
The hardest.

204
00:10:05,134 --> 00:10:07,078
Watch the direction of the question.

205
00:10:07,098 --> 00:10:08,043
Text one.

206
00:10:08,063 --> 00:10:11,113
Claims that hot water can
freeze faster than cold water,

207
00:10:11,133 --> 00:10:14,578
known as the Mpemba effect,
have been tested many times,

208
00:10:14,598 --> 00:10:16,277
with conflicting results.

209
00:10:16,359 --> 00:10:19,731
Physicists who have reviewed these
experiments point out that outcomes can

210
00:10:19,751 --> 00:10:22,339
depend on small details
that are hard to control,

211
00:10:22,359 --> 00:10:27,267
such as exactly where a thermometer is
placed and how much water evaporates as

212
00:10:27,287 --> 00:10:28,234
it cools.

213
00:10:28,254 --> 00:10:29,576
Text two.

214
00:10:29,897 --> 00:10:34,163
In a classroom demonstration, a teacher
filled one cup with hot water and an

215
00:10:34,183 --> 00:10:38,539
identical cup with cold water
and set both in the same freezer.

216
00:10:38,646 --> 00:10:40,539
The hot water froze first.

217
00:10:40,718 --> 00:10:44,806
The teacher told the class that the
demonstration proved that hot water always

218
00:10:44,826 --> 00:10:50,040
freezes faster than cold water.

219
00:10:50,078 --> 00:10:54,701
Based on the texts, how would the physicists
in Text one most likely respond to

220
00:10:54,721 --> 00:10:57,257
the teacher's conclusion in Text two?

221
00:10:57,292 --> 00:10:58,522
Pause and try it.

222
00:10:58,542 --> 00:11:00,076
Take a little longer on this one.

223
00:11:04,288 --> 00:11:05,268
Notice the flip.

224
00:11:05,288 --> 00:11:07,731
This time, Text one responds to Text two,

225
00:11:07,751 --> 00:11:10,982
so the answer has to be what
the physicists would say.

226
00:11:11,002 --> 00:11:15,804
Their line: experiments conflict,
and results hinge on small details.

227
00:11:15,824 --> 00:11:19,966
The teacher's line: one demonstration
proves it always happens.

228
00:11:19,988 --> 00:11:22,895
The physicists would push back on always.

229
00:11:22,915 --> 00:11:25,361
One result, under one set of conditions,

230
00:11:25,381 --> 00:11:27,681
can't show what always happens.

231
00:11:27,701 --> 00:11:28,903
That's choice B.

232
00:11:28,923 --> 00:11:30,652
C is the swapped trap.

233
00:11:30,672 --> 00:11:34,066
That's the teacher's view: the
demonstration settles the question.

234
00:11:34,173 --> 00:11:36,367
A is too extreme the other way.

235
00:11:36,387 --> 00:11:38,582
And D is too specific.

236
00:11:38,602 --> 00:11:40,795
Text two never mentions a thermometer,

237
00:11:40,815 --> 00:11:43,990
and the physicists can't know
what happened in that classroom.

238
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:44,633
Here's your routine.

239
00:11:44,653 --> 00:11:48,582
Read the question first, and
note who is responding to whom.

240
00:11:48,974 --> 00:11:52,812
Then read Text one and sum up
its main claim in one line.

241
00:11:52,832 --> 00:11:54,484
Do the same for Text two.

242
00:11:54,504 --> 00:11:57,627
Name the relationship: agrees, disagrees,

243
00:11:57,647 --> 00:12:00,254
qualifies, extends, or explains.

244
00:12:00,325 --> 00:12:03,075
Then check each choice twice.

245
00:12:03,147 --> 00:12:04,876
Does it match the relationship?

246
00:12:04,896 --> 00:12:07,682
And does every detail
belong to the right text?

247
00:12:07,765 --> 00:12:11,781
If Text two has a but or a however in it,

248
00:12:11,801 --> 00:12:12,566
slow down.

249
00:12:12,586 --> 00:12:14,923
That's often where
partial agreement lives,

250
